.\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & k\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nApply $R_3\\leftarrow R_3-(1)R_1$.\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & k - 7\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nApply $R_3\\leftarrow R_3-(-1)R_2$.\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 10\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe last row represents $0x_1+0x_2=k - 10$. Consistency therefore requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 &=k - 10 \\\\ k &=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot equations give $x_2=-3$ and $x_1=4$. These values also establish sufficiency:\n\n$$\n(4)\\mathbf{a}_1+(-3)\\mathbf{a}_2=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=10$ is the only value for which the vector lies in the span."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Such That a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"}]}
